      "role": "user",
      "content": "That is a very definitive stance. If I'm to use this in my report, I'll need to be able to address the counter-arguments from the technical experts at these major labs. \n\nMany of the researchers proposing these frameworks argue that the scale and complexity of frontier models create unique, unprecedented risks—such as loss of control or autonomous weaponization—that simply cannot be managed through decentralized, open-source methods without risking global catastrophe. They would argue that their \"barriers to entry\" are actually essential safety checkpoints, much like the rigorous licensing required for nuclear energy or pharmaceutical development.\n\nHow do you reconcile the possibility that these frameworks are actually, in fact, technically necessary to prevent existential harm, rather than just being a \"facade\" for market dominance? Is it possible for a regulation to be both a valid safety measure and a tool for protectionism, or do you believe the technical necessity is fundamentally manufactured?",
